# Script header and --help

## Header comment (every `.sh`)

Right after the shebang (and `set -euo pipefail` if present), a short comment block:

- What the script does, in one or two lines
- Who runs it or when (user command, `devbox.sh`/`desktop.sh` step, sourced by X, opt-in)
- Anything that surprises: touches `/etc`, rewrites tool config, needs tmux/jq/sudo

```bash
#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Install or upgrade bat from GitHub releases.
# Invoked by devbox.sh and install-tools.sh; Fedora uses dnf instead.
set -euo pipefail
```

## Executable scripts: `usage()` and `-h, --help`

Anything a user can run (top-level `*.sh`, `scripts/*.sh`, `lazyvim*/`, `server/`, `scripts/dotfiles`):

- `usage()` prints `Usage: ./path/as/typed.sh [options]`, a short description, what it
  changes, requirements, and an `Options:` list that includes `-h, --help`.
- Parse `-h|--help` before anything with side effects (sudo, network, writes). Print usage
  to stdout and `exit 0`.
- Unknown option: `Unknown option: X` plus usage on stderr, `exit 1`.
- Installers get `--uninstall`, `--dry-run`, `--yes` from `scripts/lib/install-cli.sh`;
  no-option scripts use `df_no_args_or_help` from `scripts/lib/cli-args.sh`.
- Wrappers (`server.sh`, `setup-tmux-fetch.sh`) `exec` the real script with `"$@"` so
  `--help` flows through; their header says "Compatibility wrapper".
- When options change, update everything listed in the `tldr-dotfiles` rule (CLI help, tldr page, README), not only `usage()`.
- Do not intercept `--help` in a script that must forward every argument to another
  program (`scripts/ai-agent-sudo/sudo` passes everything to the real `sudo`).

## Libraries and sourced files

- `scripts/lib/*.sh`, `lazyvim*/lib/*.sh`: header only (no `--help`). State what the caller
  must define, e.g. `# Requires: log, run, DRY_RUN`.
- `home/**` shell files that are sourced (prompts, aliases, profile): header only.

## Verify without installing

Run only `--help`, with stubs on `PATH` for `sudo`/`apt`/`dnf`/`curl`/`git` and a throwaway
`HOME`. It must print `Usage` and exit 0 with nothing blocked.

```bash
env -i HOME=/tmp/scratch PATH=/tmp/stubs:/usr/bin:/bin bash ./scripts/foo-install-update.sh --help </dev/null
```
